#edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b7066b5-7785-5214-82a7-4a179f269b09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176634 #edb/ref 1061 1897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176634 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "660295f1-95d1-56e0-b591-fce238e8c6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176634 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176687 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176687 #edb/ref 1061 28638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176687 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7c95bae-98c7-5473-ab7a-fd3e93fc1513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176687 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176790 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176790 #edb/ref 1061 34943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176790 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93de155f-ab7f-5540-9acb-23b205726c7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176790 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176792 #edb/ref 1061 35018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48e7c710-7513-58d4-b6e5-f52fee9a3d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177026 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x+y+1, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nOur goal here is to approximate $y(1)$ *and* $y(2)$ using Euler's method. \n\n![](images/88/88b9a68dd2f5982b14a1d16588320a4770612eacdd2f1915a5e659c4a89b9760.png)\n\nWe will proceed using two steps of equal size. The step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}{2}}-{\\color{red}{0}}}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of all the numbers. Let's start by adding the initial condition data $y(0) = 2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nWe now proceed in two steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute $y'$ using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row of the table, and we calculate $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =0+2+1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+3 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**: We now repeat the process. We compute $y'$ using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *second* row of the table, and we calculate $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =x+y+1 \\\\  & =1+5+1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find new values for $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =5+7 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1)\\approx 5$ and $y(2)\\approx 12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177026 #edb/ref 1065 5690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "139fbc6d-df3c-58ad-9e25-073c549a161d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177026 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177098 #edb/ref 1064 "When applying Euler's method, an important point to keep in mind is that the smaller the step size is, the better our approximation will be. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the graph below.\n\n![](images/80/80d62e063823096c1ba6896227ff8d03f10786d1117d70002985e78a14a46655.png)\n\nThe solid curve represents the actual solution $y = y(x)$ to an initial value problem with initial condition $y(x_0) = y_0.$ The dotted curves represent some Euler's approximations to the same initial value problem:\n\n- The blue dots represent the approximation obtained using Euler's method with $\\Delta x = 0.05.$\n\n- The red dots represent the approximation obtained using Euler's method with $\\Delta x = 0.25.$\n\nThe diagram shows that the sequence of points with the smaller step size approximates the actual solution better than the sequence with the larger step size." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177098 #edb/ref 1065 969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a9ac2e4e-052b-51b2-9f41-ee7bac19f763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Importance of Step Size in Euler's Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428147 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428147 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 2 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1, 2]; waits [1, 1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (2/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1179->1061. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-rel