ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547962 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee13cf7b-506a-556c-9c39-4af06c9d75a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547963 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547963 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547963 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547963 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef950b23-df29-5428-9d9a-cb0f2b045309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186547969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547968 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "fb209e62-dbab-5627-b8de-306596211c79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186548188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1085 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $y=f(x)$ be the solution that satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "606e3887-aa6c-5661-a09f-d03aff0342a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547969 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186548138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $|C|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ use the general solution to the equation to find the function $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11ed723f-8fe2-5c56-8bb0-841acdfb0940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547977 #edb/ref 1074 "The general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{2}+C) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(C) \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of ${y}^{′}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation. Express your answer in the form $y=y(x).$\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C),\\,\\,$where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bc80b6f-e02e-58f0-97b4-8ea651af23ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547982 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $y=y(x)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f6c3d65-e721-5449-9ddc-958a81a54048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547987 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nWriting the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{9}+⋯\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!},\n$$\n\ncalculate the radius of convergence $R$ of this power series.\n\n$R\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8679792d-1ee6-50ba-bf59-17896dc979de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548024 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(0.2)$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b186f94-2456-5e8b-8019-128c4ccb5b71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548034 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(0.2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=0.2$ is $0.2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0.2-0}{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 0.1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+0.1=0.1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0.1\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0.2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0.2\\cdot 0.1=0.02 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0.1+0.1=0.2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0.02=0.02\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.02$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.02$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $f(0.2)≈0.02.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126768" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x),$ approximate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Write your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\,≈\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3b460f4-8f03-59ea-8d4e-3d88c9d8d99f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548037 #edb/ref 1074 "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ by integrating the above series term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{42}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{42}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{″}(0).$\n\n${f}^{″}(0)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4bf276d-b40b-5d47-b840-e4ace9d966fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548038 #edb/ref 1074 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot 2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4{x}^{2}y+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the point $(0,0)$ lies on $f,$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & =-4\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+2\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1d89a0f-00b0-5608-ac35-e4c5c811a70d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548058 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with ${x}^{2}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}) & =({x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2})^{3}+\\frac{1}{120}({x}^{2})^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548083 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "019385a6-ffb0-5ec8-b099-e4938f797fc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186548058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548088 #edb/ref 1062 #